{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1552542-the-flawed-vision-of-the-malkavian-dream","id":1552542,"title":"The Flawed Vision of the Malkavian Dream","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-07-20T06:54:20-07:00","id":1552542,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> The Flawed Vision of the Malkavian Dream","content":"Heyo!<br /> <br /> Today I come here with an issue that I encountered, and while I think myself somewhat fluent in the lore of the setting, examples escape me. The Issue is that a Malkavian character has become too reliant on their Sight (They are the classical oracle Malkav in terms of capability) and their Sponsor is seeking to make them realize that the Sight can A. be flawed or even wrong, and B. While it can serve as motivation, it is not acceptable as justification. (It can motivate you to search for evidence of treachery, but very few would accept you stabbing your coterie mate over a vision)<br /> <br /> What I wondered was if there was any salient example one could point to illustrate this ? Perhaps it is my own dislike towards the idea of absolute determinism, but it feels hard to believe that the Madness Network\u00b4s greatest flaw in function is that it does not distinguish between salient and irrelevant information.<br /> <br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28457-xasure","name":"Xasure","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"14"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-20T07:27:26-07:00","id":1552543,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure whether you mean examples from the lore or just anywhere. The issue of fate (and determinism or its falsity) is vastly explored in most theologies and many philosophies. The issue of divination and its implications, same.<br /> <br /> You first have to decide what is the reality of fate and divination in your world, and then how Malkavian Sight specifically relates to it.<br /> Then you have to decide what the cultural context the characters live in takes as true regarding divination and its forms. This will dictate different responses from characters regardless of whether the insights are true or false.<br /> <br /> For example, in the real world, in the Islamic tradition, dreams and intuitions have great importance to individual spirituality, which is sanctioned even by orthodox theology. Manuals of creed and jurisprudence have chapters devoted to dreams and their interpretation. However, it is also an established principle that they are not acceptable evidence to change a religious ruling or to influence conclusively a judge's verdict. Ex.: even if a person of utter renown for his erudition, piety and truthful predictions comes up and says he has dreamt the Prophet - peace and blessings be upon him - himself (a particularly indisputable vision because by creed the devils cannot imitate his form in dreams) telling him that alcohol is now lawful for him, this will be rejected automatically as his own confusion on what he has seen. Same if he were to claim that he knows who killed someone else based on dream or intuition: it would not be admissible in court, even though people might have grounds to trust him, and yet he may even told off for <i>relaying</i> his dream because as it stands is akin to accusing someone without evidence and tarnishing his reputation.<br /> <br /> Other traditions, like the Catholic one, or I'm sure with other religions that I am less familiar with, have rules laid out for these things. Of course, oracle business was especially important in ancient religions and divination was often regulated and had standards (but there were also big debates on whethere fate was deterministic or not and whether this or that form of divination was legit or the craft of charlatans). Granted, people with less religious education might not be well informed on these matters and react to such claims based on emotion and guesswork rather than doctrine, but there are still rules in place.<br /> <br /> Consider all this for your world, and then it should be easy to decide how to handle this specific character. Maybe in your particular city there is already a system in place. Maybe one character in the coterie could stand by the Malkavian's side like a fanatic follower while another can't stand his divinatory nonsense and will put a stake through his heart in a moment if he does anything too crazy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}